Software Project Management Plan

**TradeSage AI**

*CE363: Project-III | CHARUSAT*

# 1. Introduction

## 1.1 Purpose

This document defines the management approach for TradeSage AI: schedule, effort allocation, risk register, and monitoring plan across the project's 60 allotted lab hours (CE363, solo development).

## 1.2 Project Overview

TradeSage AI is developed by a single student across four phases: Setup & Documentation, MVP Core, ML Depth & Credibility Layer, and Polish/Stretch Goals — as defined in the locked project specification.

# 2. Project Organization

Solo development. One person holds all roles: Developer, Tester, and (within the application itself) both the Trader/User and Admin actors. No team-coordination overhead, but no redundancy either — schedule risk from illness, blockers, or scope misjudgment falls entirely on one person, so slack time is built into the schedule below rather than assumed away.

# 3. Effort Estimate (60 Lab Hours)

| **Phase** | **Focus** | **Hours** | **% of Total** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Phase 0 | Setup & Documentation | 6 | 10% |
| Phase 1 | MVP Core (Auth, Portfolio, baseline Signal Engine) | 16 | 27% |
| Phase 2 | ML Depth (sentiment, LSTM, backtesting, explainability) | 26 | 43% |
| Phase 3 | Stretch goals (Watchlist, Admin panel) — if time allows | 8 | 13% |
| Buffer | Contingency (debugging, integration issues, report writing) | 4 | 7% |

Phase 2 gets the largest share deliberately — it's the part most likely to overrun (model tuning, aligning sentiment + price data, backtest correctness) and it's what makes the ML claims in the report defensible. If time runs short, Phase 3 is cut first, not Phase 2.

# 4. Schedule / Milestones

| **Milestone** | **Target** | **Deliverable** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| M1 | End of Phase 0 | Synopsis, SRS, SPMP finalized; repo + coding-standard boilerplate ready |
| M2 | End of Phase 1 | Working MVP: auth, portfolio tracking, baseline signal (no sentiment/LSTM yet) |
| M3 | Mid Phase 2 | Sentiment pipeline + LSTM integrated, compared against baseline |
| M4 | End of Phase 2 | Backtesting module complete (Sharpe/win-rate/drawdown); Limitations section drafted |
| M5 | End of Phase 3 (if reached) | Stretch goals implemented, or explicitly deferred with justification in report |
| M6 | Final | User testing (2–3 traders, CO6), Final Report, PPT complete |

# 5. Risk Management (Risk Register)

| **Risk** | **Likelihood** | **Impact** | **Mitigation** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Low signal accuracy / weak sentiment-price correlation | High | Medium | Framed from the start as expected — evaluation uses Sharpe/win-rate/drawdown, not accuracy, so a “weak” directional signal is still a valid, honestly-reported result |
| Free-tier API rate limits (yfinance, NewsAPI, Reddit) | Medium | Medium | Cache responses locally; design refresh schedule around limits, not around ideal frequency |
| Solo schedule risk (no redundancy if blocked) | Medium | High | Buffer hours reserved (Section 3); Phase 3 is the pressure valve — cut first, not Phase 2 |
| LSTM/GRU training complexity underestimated | Medium | High | Baseline model (Logistic Regression/Random Forest) ships first in Phase 1, so there’s always a working fallback even if LSTM integration slips |
| Non-stationarity of financial data degrades model over time | High | Low (for project scope) | Explicitly documented as a known limitation, not hidden — protects report credibility per the Honesty Framework |

# 6. Monitoring & Control Plan

- Weekly progress log maintained for internal guide review (lab hours)

- Each milestone (M1–M6) reviewed against actual hours spent vs. estimate; if a phase overruns by more than ~20%, remaining Phase 3 scope is reassessed immediately rather than at the end

- Coding Standard (CHARUSAT v1.0) compliance checked at each module completion, not deferred to a final cleanup pass

# 7. Quality Assurance

All code follows the CHARUSAT Coding Standard v1.0 (naming conventions, module headers). Every module includes a header documenting name, date, author, modification history, synopsis, and functions/I-O params, per course requirement.